From 0b119e5ea5fe5ed0890c97dae465e7d2175c5246 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObeseCatLord <6099645+ObeseCatLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:07:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] iop: contain synchronous RPC failures to the failing call
 (H-IOP3, H-IOP13)

An unrelated fault while a synchronous sceSifCallRpc was in flight used to
latch the whole IOP (resetRequired). Every way the call can fail now fails
only that call: the EE receives -5 (new IopGuestCallStatus::Failed, logged
with the server function) and the IOP keeps running.

- budget exhaustion no longer latches: the absolute deadline stops the
  server CPU and any nested callback/scheduler slice, then the pump returns
- a fault or unimplemented import in another IOP thread or callback is no
  longer escalated; only the RPC server's own fault/missing import fails
  its call
- a competing waiter on the same semaphore no longer fails the call: the
  detached RPC wait joins the wait queue ordered by (priority, arrival)
  instead of the lowest thread id
- a deleted semaphore, a nested-context wait and a pump failure fail the call
- a CPU exception now stops that CPU (the exception vector is empty RAM, the
  old behaviour executed whatever followed) and is logged once
- nested host-initiated calls beyond the private call-stack arena are
  refused instead of borrowing the suspended thread's stack (H-IOP13)

The RPC model itself (server function on a private CPU while the EE is
blocked) is unchanged; see the report for why running it on the registered
IOP thread needs asynchronous EE-side completion first.

Tests: iop_rpc_wait_boundaries.inc rewritten for the new contract (competing
waiter, unrelated thread fault/missing import, own fault/missing import,
budget, nested deadline, recursion refusal, failed-then-healthy RPC).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
